    "body": "Nate Watson’s case against the big supply chain software rollout comes down to timing. A team can spend 18 months spelling out its processes for a new system, he said, and its operations and partners will have changed by the time the system goes live. The time to value just doesn’t make sense.\nWatson is chief commercial officer of Vantage 9 , a Bentonville, Arkansas, company that sells what it calls a composable supply chain platform. Its warehousing, inventory, order, yard and transportation functions come as prebuilt components that run on top of a company’s existing ERP, warehouse, and transportation management systems.\nHe made the argument in a FreightWaves Today interview on Oct. 28 at F3: Future of Freight Festival in Chattanooga, Tennessee, where AI adoption was one of the main themes on the agenda. Speakers kept returning to the same pressures, Watson said: AI, disruption in software-as-a-service and customers who expect their data to be integrated and secure.\n“An 18 or 24 month supply chain system project just doesn’t make sense given the pace of change,” Watson said. “In talking with supply chain leaders, it’s clear that this approach is slow, often runs late, is expensive, and pulls staff away from their day jobs.”\nThe trouble starts in planning. “You work for 18 months saying, here’s my process. Here’s what I would need the system to do. Here’s the data and the reporting that I would need,” Watson said. “Well, 18 months in a supply chain is a lifetime. So you get to that go-live and then all of a sudden your operations have changed, your partners have changed.”\nCustom code or a second vendor\nA company that needs its ERP to handle 10% more than it does today has two options, Watson said: customize the system, which takes time and often specialized staff, or buy the missing capability from another vendor and run two products that do not communicate well.\n“Customization is synonymous with expensive and slow,” Watson said. “Being able to deliver the value of custom at the speed of Saas is the problem Vantage 9 is out to solve.” Vantage 9 starts from the capabilities a customer needs, with the data flow and integrations already in place, so its team can concentrate on the screens operators will use.\n“Everyone’s talked about an iterative and agile approach for years now,” Watson said. “It’s nothing new, but having the systems behind it that allow you to deliver that capability is new and is emerging.”\nPrebuilt pieces on top of existing systems\nVantage 9 breaks the workload of the large systems into smaller components and assembles them for each customer.\n“You can think about it like a Lego set,” Watson said. “We’ve broken down inventory management, order management, yard, warehousing, transportation functionality that you would turn to your systems for, and broken those down into individual capabilities, like a Lego piece.”\nA large retailer might need three different warehouse management setups, Watson said, depending on the type of building and the partners each one serves. Each could draw on inventory functions and connect to the retailer’s transportation system or outside partners.\nCustomers can keep the systems they already run.\n“If you have a system of record that’s doing the job 80 percent, that’s great,” Watson said. “With a composable platform, you’re able to leverage the very best of the systems that you already have in place, and then add in that 5, 10 percent where it’s missing.”\nHis example is a retailer that reconfigures a warehouse to handle product at a new temperature. The legacy warehouse management system may not have been designed for a last-in, first-out setup or for monitoring temperature changes. Vantage 9 would add the data management and the sensor integrations alongside it, he said, and each transportation provider or supplier added after that becomes easier to connect.\nA company can also start with one warehouse type in one region and test it there without changing the system that manages the rest of the network.\nSpreading the go-live out\nSupply chains run 24 hours a day, seven days a week, and they cannot shut down for a technology upgrade, Watson said.\n“So much of the challenge with these large system implementations is the people side. It’s the adoption. It’s slowing your operations so that you can go have a go-live for a new system or roll out new functionality,” he said.\nA traditional cutover packs that change into one day: in Watson’s words, “you leave work Friday, work in one way, you show up Monday.” Adding capability in smaller steps spreads the training and change management over time and limits the disruption customers see, he said.\n“If you have a big go-live and that’s disruptive to customers, it can be months before you regain that trust. That’s a business killer,” Watson said.\nVantage 9’s implementation team is made up of people who have worked in warehousing and transportation, Watson said, and it spends time on the floor learning “where’s the pallet loading, who’s running the dock.” That knowledge goes into the last 10% of each project, the interface warehouse staff actually work in.\nOne layer for people and AI agents\nWatson sees the composable platform as the place where AI agents fit in. An executive might run a single process across five systems, he said, and what that executive cares about is the process and the outcome. The platform sits across those tools and sets data access, governance and permissions in one place.\n“So whether it’s a human warehouse manager or their agent working on their behalf, our view is that in a composable platform, access to that data, the governance around that, the permissions, all needs to be set at a layer that’s not system-specific, that you can trade in and out those specific systems as needed as you get better capabilities from another,” he said.\nFrom Firebend to Vantage 9\nThe company started in 2019 as Firebend, launched by a team of supply chain operators and technologists from Fort Smith, Arkansas, and spun out of Propak Corp.\nVantage 9 says its deployments are built to reach measurable return on investment within 90 days. The company says an unnamed national retailer saved more than $6 million in the first fiscal year, and it lists a 90% reduction in gate processing time and a 700% improvement in unload times among its results.\nTwo questions before a big purchase\nAsked what an executive should consider before a large supply chain technology investment, Watson started with whether a new system is needed at all. The first question is whether an existing system can be modified or enhanced, and whether that saves money or time or helps the team more than a replacement would.\n“Oftentimes we think, I don’t have the budget or the time to replace my system. But, if you can leverage what works well within you existing system, your focus becomes on small improvements that are faster and more cost effective.” The second is what sets the company up to keep improving over time.\n“No one knows what technology is going to look like in two years, five years, ten years’ time,” Watson said. “Being in a posture where it’s easy for you to bring in the latest and best, but have that context of your organization and that data layer in place so that you can leverage new tools as they come out, allows you to experiment and innovate faster.”\nLearn more about Vantage 9.",
